### Encapsulation, Echo Service, and DPP
* **Presentation**: [BIBE, Echo Service, DPP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dtn-sessb-bibe-echo-service-dpp-01)
* **Presenter**: Rick Taylor

* **Bundle-in-Bundle Encapsulation (`draft-ietf-dtn-bibe`)**:
  * Alberto Montilla joined as co-editor.
  * The draft addresses interaction issues between RFC 9171 fragmentation and Bundle Protocol Security (BPSec).
  * Scope is strictly limited to Bundle Protocol Version 7 (BPv7) in BPv7 encapsulation; wire format remains identical to the CCSDS specification.
  * Scoping was tightened around source/destination transfer tuples, canonical requirement phrases, and lifetime propagation to outer bundles.
  * Text was added allowing Anycast addresses for tunnel endpoints under specific stable-contact scenarios (e.g., ground station to spacecraft flyovers).
  * Aggregation in tunneling will be discussed in the Friday session.

* **BPv7 Echo Service (`draft-ietf-dtn-echo-service`)**:
  * Provides a standardized echo mechanism (ping) for OAM and management across BPv7 implementations.
  * Revision `-00` incorporates operational considerations (handling non-responses, loop prevention based on feedback from Eric Klein and Edward Birrane).
  * Updated IANA registry request to use `TBD1` from the well-known service number space.
  * Added an appendix on Path MTU (PMTU) discovery and path probing using status reports and unknown extension blocks.

* **Delay-Tolerant Peer-to-Peer Protocol (DPP)**:
  * Personal draft aiming to provide inter-domain routing between separate administrative domains (e.g., space agencies).
  * Uses BGP-like peering concepts built over gRPC/TLS with identity rooted in DNS.
  * Rick Taylor requested community review and noted plans to mature the implementation before requesting adoption prior to IETF 128.

### CoAP over BP
* **Presentation**: [CoAP ove BP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dtn-sessb-coap-ove-bp-00)
* **Presenter**: Anna Calveras (co-authored with Carles Gomez and Mahitab ElAttar)
* **Updates in `draft-ietf-dtn-coap-over-bp` (version 05)**:
  * Scope is restricted strictly to the 3-element IPN scheme (`ipn:`); the `dtn:` scheme is out of scope.
  * Updated URI scheme and authority to use `.alt` (`ipn.alt`) instead of `.arpa` since DNS is not required, removing the IANA reservation request for `ipn.arpa`.
  * Corrected the bundle lifetime formula for aggregated messages to account for maximum aggregation delay.
  * Set default parameter `NSTART` to be strictly greater than 1 when aggregating messages, with a warning to manage congestion.
  * Updated the NON timeout random value formulation to align with RFC 9177.
  * A reference implementation tested during the IETF Hackathon was made available on GitHub. Presentation to the Core WG is also planned.

### DTN Adopted Status Updates
* **Presentation**: [DTN Adopted Status - Sipos](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dtn-sessb-dtn-adopted-status-sipos-00)
* **Presenter**: Brian Sipos

* **BPSec COSE Security Context (`draft-ietf-dtn-cose-context`)**:
  * Passed AD review; revisions addressed document candidate shipper and AD feedback (added interop minimums, nonce explanations, OCSP operational considerations, CA/Browser Forum and CCSDS references, fixed examples).
  * Currently in IETF Last Call. Issue #150 covers comparison to European Cybersecurity Certification Group (ECCG) recommendations, informative references for post-quantum/hybrid PQT registrations, and adding MLDSA-65 to interop minimums.
  * Éric Vyncke requested uploading an updated draft revision containing the current repository fixes to assist Last Call reviewers.
  * Rick Taylor raised a gap analysis between COSE context and RFC 9173 regarding IV/ciphertext handling; discussion touched on potentially obsoleting RFC 9173 in the future.

* **Bundle Protocol Endpoint ID Patterns (`draft-ietf-dtn-eid-pattern`)**:
  * Updated to version 11. Introduces an optional leading pipe character (`|`) in text format to explicitly distinguish pattern strings from standard URIs.
  * Restricted scheme-specific parts to exclude non-printable characters and spaces.
  * Explicitly updates COSE context PKIX usage for Subject Alternative Names. Added operational guidance on pattern structure options. Brian Sipos will request WGLC on the mailing list.

* **UDP Convergence Layer Protocol Version 2 (`draft-ietf-dtn-udpcl`)**:
  * No text changes since last meeting; trial implementations exist.
  * Requires IP multicast TBD assignments from IANA (requesting AD assistance).
  * Open Issue #33: Discussing whether to request an ALPN assignment for DTLS (supported by Eric Klein to facilitate running over port 443). Brian Sipos will request early allocation and WGLC.

* **Secure Advertisement and Neighborhood Discovery (`draft-ietf-dtn-bp-sand`)**:
  * Updated to version 05. Added explicit use case illustrations.
  * Defined "fully specified Convergence Layer (CL) type" to enable zero-configuration setup (e.g., explicit LTP lower-layer parameters).
  * Removed resource and router advertisements because they were not required by the identified use cases.

### DTNMA Application Data Model (ADM) YANG Syntax
* **Presentation**: [ADM YANG](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dtn-sessb-adm-yang-01)
* **Presenter**: Per Andersson (co-authored with Brian Sipos)
* **Updates in `draft-ietf-dtn-adm-yang` (version 08)**:
  * Refactored data models to use native YANG constructs wherever possible instead of custom YANG extensions.
  * Built-in AMM types now map directly to derived YANG typedefs (e.g., UVAS mapped to a derived `uint64`). `REAL32` and `REAL64` lack native YANG types and maintain custom encodings.
  * Replaced custom AMM `type` and `base` extensions with native YANG `identity` and `base` keywords.
  * Replaced Externally Defined Data (EDD) elements with native `config false` leaves.
  * Brian Sipos confirmed toolchains and ADM definitions are being updated to match.
  * Next step is to request an early YANG Doctors review (Per Andersson will recuse himself as YANG Doctor secretary).

### Chat Log Discussion: Scope of BP vs. TIPTOP WG
* In the chat, Behcet Sarikaya inquired why Bundle Protocol (BP) was not adopted by the TIPTOP Working Group for space communications.
* Rick Taylor, Éric Vyncke, Jen Linkova, and Jorge Amodio clarified that DTN/BP and TIPTOP have distinct charters and use cases: TIPTOP focuses on extending IP and QUIC to deep space, while BP/DTN is out of scope for TIPTOP.

